> > > effective at it.  By the very late game the pattern to use
> > > on advancing monsters can be cast, zap, fire all before
> > > melee.

> > Agreed.  If nothing else, I like to get cure sickness at 0%
> > failure rate before the planes (and Valks can do this).  But
> > magic missle, cone of cold, etc., are a great alternative to
> > slinging daggers as well.

> To me not just an alternative but a supplement.  Cast at a
> distance as long as energy and nutrition is available, fire
> daggers once in range.  At some point I measured throwing and
> casting difference and it seemed like I could cast/zap a bit
> farther than I could throw.  I may have gotten that distance
> estimate wrong.

I meant the word alternative in its most general sense.  It's
another choice available.  When I have it, I'll use it if I run
out of daggers (doh), or if there's water around, or for various
other reasons.  I'd not noticed the difference in effective
distance, but then, I've never really paid that much attention.

I pretty much agree with your division of phases, and your
analysis (which I've cut), with one exception---even in
mid-game, if I've not found good spell friendly armor, I'll not
be using many spells.  Although this depends on the spells: some
are quite useful in "safe" locations, taking off your armor to
use, then putting it back on---these can (and will) get
exercised.  Attack spells, on the other hand, are really only
useful when there's a monster in view.  And I'm not about to
take off any armor (except perhaps a shield) then.  Especially
as by that time, the hindering body armor is under a cloak of
some type, so taking it off practically means stripping.

Of course, as usual, it all depends.  +3 studded leather is as
good as +0 dwarvish mithral, and once you've found Ludios,
there's a decent chance of getting it.

